From 5657c418a270785136918a1168e1fa3acacdb39b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bsp2020 <bsp2020@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 05:39:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] cuda: fuse DSV4_HC_POST -> RMS_NORM -> MUL and let qwen4exp
 producers leave the BF16 copy

Under -sm tensor each qwen4exp layer ends with the DSV4_HC_POST op (the
unfused hc chain is not correct under the Meta backend), and the next layer
normalizes its output with RMS_NORM + MUL(gamma): two kernels and an extra
read of the hc-wide stream. One kernel now computes the hc_post sum with the
same statements as dsv4_hc_post_f32, stores it (the next residual), and does
the scalar-path reduction, scale and gamma product of rms_norm_f32, so the
output is bit-identical. The graph optimizer keeps x / residual / post alive
until the MUL (other blocks still read them while xn is written); an overlap
check falls back to the unfused kernels, and the fusion stands down under
GGML_CUDA_RMS_NORM_VEC=1. GGML_CUDA_HC_POST_RMS=0 turns it off.

The normalized stream (and the dsv4_hc_pre output) feed BF16 GEMMs that run
on rocBLAS on RDNA4, each of which first converts the whole activation to
BF16. dsv4_hc_post_rms and dsv4_hc_pre now also write the RNE BF16 copy of
their output in the same pass and leave it in bf16_src1_cache, where
ggml_cuda_mul_mat_cublas_impl finds it. The F32 output is always written; the
gates match the lookup's (T >= 64, contiguous root tensor), so decode is
untouched. GGML_CUDA_BF16_PRODUCER=0 turns this off; =2 re-converts every
cache hit and compares (skipped inside a graph capture).

2x R9700, -sm tensor, Flash-Next IQ3_S, r235 host-pool settings: prefill
31.5k 2143 -> 2263 t/s (+5.6 %), 101k 2088 -> 2214 (+5.9 %); greedy / 4.4k
prompt set / 101k hashes unchanged in every arm; =2 over the same prompts:
32,501 hits, 0 mismatching elements.
